## 6. Key Design Decisions & Trade-Offs

### 6.1 Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| AOP in `shared` module | Cross-cutting concern. Every module has access. Keeps domain pure. |
| L2CacheProvider as interface in `shared` | Dependency inversion. `shared` defines contract; `outbound/repository/cache` implements it. Allows testing with mock L2. |
| Nullable L2CacheProvider in Aspect | Modules that don't include `outbound/repository/cache` (e.g., unit tests, L1-only deployments) degrade gracefully. |
| Jackson for L2 serialization | Project already uses Jackson. Valkey stores `String` values. JSON is human-readable and debuggable in Valkey CLI. |
| SpEL for key expressions | Consistent with Spring Cache conventions. Developers already familiar. Supports complex key logic. |
| Caffeine per-cacheName instances | Different caches need different TTLs and max sizes. A shared Caffeine instance cannot support heterogeneous TTLs. |
| Sorted list keys | `#brandIds.sorted().joinToString(',')` ensures [1,2,3] and [3,1,2] produce the same cache key. |

### 6.2 Trade-Offs

| Trade-Off | Pro | Con | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|---|
| AOP proxy limitation | Simple annotation usage | Only works on Spring-managed beans, not self-invocation | Document this. If self-invocation needed, inject the proxy. |
| Jackson serialization for L2 | Human-readable, debuggable | Slightly larger than binary (Kryo, Protobuf) | Acceptable for this use case. JSON overhead is marginal for domain objects. |
| `shared` module gaining AOP dependency | Central, reusable | `shared` becomes slightly heavier | AOP starter is lightweight. Already has Spring deps. |
| Per-cacheName Caffeine instances | Correct TTL per cache | Memory overhead of multiple Caffeine instances | ConcurrentHashMap lazy-creates. Typically fewer than 20 cache names in practice. |
| No async L2 writes | Simplicity | L2 write latency added to response | For proxy API data, L2 write is <1ms network hop. Can add async later if metrics justify. |

