## 7. Error Handling & Resilience

### 7.1 L2 (Valkey) Failure Patterns

```
Scenario 1: L2 GET fails (network timeout, connection pool exhausted)
  --> runCatching returns null
  --> Treated as L2 MISS
  --> Actual method executed, result cached in L1 only
  --> Logged at ERROR level

Scenario 2: L2 PUT fails (write after method execution)
  --> runCatching swallows exception
  --> Result still returned to caller (already in L1)
  --> Logged at ERROR level
  --> Next request will re-populate L2

Scenario 3: L2 entirely unavailable (Valkey cluster down)
  --> All L2 operations fail silently
  --> System operates in L1-only mode
  --> Performance degrades but functionality is preserved

Scenario 4: L2 returns corrupted/incompatible JSON
  --> Deserialization fails in runCatching
  --> Treated as L2 MISS
  --> Fresh data fetched and written to L2 (overwrites bad data)
```

### 7.2 L1 (Caffeine) Failure Patterns

Caffeine is in-process and virtually never fails. However:

```
Scenario: JVM memory pressure causes evictions
  --> Caffeine's size-based eviction handles this automatically
  --> Falls through to L2 or actual method
  --> No error handling needed (by design)
```

### 7.3 Circuit Breaker Consideration (Future Enhancement)

If Valkey failures become frequent, a circuit breaker pattern can be added:

```kotlin
// Future: wrap L2CacheProvider with circuit breaker
class ResilientL2CacheProvider(
    private val delegate: L2CacheProvider,
) : L2CacheProvider {
    private val circuitBreaker = CircuitBreaker(
        failureThreshold = 5,
        resetTimeoutMs = 30_000,
    )
    // ...
}
```

This is NOT included in the initial implementation because:
- The existing `runCatching` pattern provides sufficient resilience
- Adding a circuit breaker adds complexity
- Should be data-driven: add when Valkey failure metrics justify it

