## 8. Cache Eviction Strategy

### 8.1 Time-Based Eviction (Primary)

| Level | Mechanism | Default |
|---|---|---|
| L1 (Caffeine) | `expireAfterWrite` | 5 minutes |
| L2 (Valkey) | Redis TTL (`SETEX`) | 1 hour |

### 8.2 Size-Based Eviction (L1 Only)

- Caffeine `maximumSize` per cache name (default: 10,000 entries)
- Eviction policy: Window TinyLfu (Caffeine default, near-optimal hit rate)

### 8.3 Explicit Eviction

- `@TieredCacheEvict(cacheName = "brand", key = "...")` for targeted eviction
- `@TieredCacheEvict(cacheName = "brand", allEntries = true)` for full cache clear
- Kafka event-driven eviction: when product data changes, handler evicts relevant cache entries

### 8.4 Consistency Model

This design uses **eventual consistency**:
- L1 may serve stale data for up to `l1TtlSeconds` after L2 is updated
- L2 may serve stale data for up to `l2TtlSeconds` after source data changes
- For the proxy API use case (brand/category/manufacturer data), this is acceptable because:
  - This data changes infrequently (hours/days between changes)
  - 5-minute L1 staleness and 1-hour L2 staleness are business-acceptable
  - Real-time consistency is not required for reference data

